 "params": "{name, color?: \"#rrggbb\" | [r, g, b], transparency?: 0-100, cut?, surface?, texture?: {kind?, size?: [w, h], angle?, offset?: [x, y], path?, image?} | null, rename?, delete?: true} a material of the project's library, created or changed: its colour in shaded 3D views, its `transparency` there (percent of what lies behind it that shows through, 0 opaque; the starter Glass has 70), the pattern its cut shows in plans and sections (where a layer is wide enough to hold it), and the one its surfaces show at its size in the building (a floor's top in plan, a ceiling's underside in its ceiling plan). Patterns: none, solid, diagonal, crosshatch, horizontal, vertical, grid, brick, concrete, insulation, or the name of one of the project's fill patterns (see `fill_pattern`), kept while the material shows it and renamed with it. Its `texture` is what its surfaces look like in the 3D view, over its colour, a tile of it `size` mm across and up, turned `angle` degrees and moved `offset` mm: a pattern drawn in code, `kind` brick, block, boards, concrete, plaster, tile, roof, fabric, metal or insulation (at its natural size unless `size` says); or `image`, a picture: the PNG or JPEG file at `path`, taken into the project (up to 1024 px a side, 24 pictures), or one the project holds (`image`, its id from `inspect`); null takes the texture away. `rename` renames it, the layers using it following; `delete` takes it away, while no layer uses it. `inspect` lists the library and the pictures"
}
{
 "aliases": [],
 "id": "make_fill_pattern",
 "label": "Make Fill Pattern",
 "menu": null,
 "params": "{ids, from: [x, y], to: [x, y], name, target?: model|drafting} a fill pattern made of the detail lines `ids` (all of one view), as pyRevit's Make Pattern makes one: the rectangle between corners `from` and `to` (in the view's terms) is the tile the pattern repeats, and what of the lines lies outside it is cut off. Each line becomes a family that runs on into the next tiles' copies of itself; one at a slope that never meets itself again is turned to the simplest slope within 0.25° that does (else the nearest, at most 48 tiles across or up). A model pattern (the default) is as drawn; a drafting one is the drawing on paper, at the view's scale. The tile is 0.05 to 100000 each way, and holds at most 256 lines. It returns the pattern's `name` and the `pattern`"
}
{
 "aliases": [
  "LW"
 ],
 "id": "linework",
 "label": "Linework",
 "menu": null,
 "params": "{view?, id, at: [x, y], style? | invisible?: true, remove?: true} Revit's Linework: the edge of element `id` that a plan (`view`, a level's or a plan view; the lowest level's by default) draws nearest `at` (within 200 mm), drawn in that view only in line style `style`, or not at all with `invisible`; `remove` takes the Linework nearest `at` off that edge again (one edge, even where another lies within reach; where that edge has none, the nearest entry within reach on an edge the element no longer draws, one a call). A view holds at most 2000 edges. It returns the edge, `from` and `to`, or with `remove` how many were `removed`"
}
{
